I’ll build this as a defensible cumulative Rietveld analysis: first inspect the public pattern/metadata and available refinement tooling, then identify coherent phase candidates, refine the inventory and nuisance model, and finally replay and cross-check the submitted GSAS-II project against the exported CIFs, fractions, and profile. I’ll keep structural coordinates fixed until the phase/profile model is stable and will report any ambiguity rather than forcing an assignment.

The public chemistry points first to Ca–W–O products, but I’m treating CaWO₄, residual CaO/WO₃, and off-stoichiometric calcium tungstates as hypotheses—not conclusions. I’m now extracting the peak families and checking the offline published-structure catalog so candidate selection is based on the measured pattern and chemically valid structures.

The peak families support a three-phase starting inventory: scheelite CaWO₄ dominates; the 23–24.5° triplet and its higher-angle companions require monoclinic WO₃; and the independent rocksalt series near 32.1, 37.4, 53.4, 64.4, and 67.4° supports residual CaO. I’m moving these into matched GSAS-II refinements, while retaining orthorhombic/triclinic WO₃ and alternative CaWO₄ parents as swap tests rather than choosing from stick patterns alone.

The strongest currently unmodeled residuals form a coherent double-perovskite-like series (about 19.35, 22.04, 31–32, 36–37, 45.14, 56–57, and 65.82°). I’m therefore testing Ca₃WO₆ models derived independently from Ca₂MgWO₆, Sr₂MnWO₆, and Ca₃UO₆ parents—preserving their distinct parent sites while mapping them to Ca—alongside CaO deletion/addition models. This is a genuine inventory test: all candidates receive the same low-flexibility background, position, cell, and profile treatment.

The first controlled comparison is decisive enough to keep working on the missing phase: the Sr₂MnWO₆-derived Ca₃WO₆ model lowers Rwp from 16.50% (CaWO₄ + WO₃) and 16.41% (adding CaO) to 13.25%, with a refined abundance near 12 wt%. The independent Mg-parent topology also improves the fit, but less strongly. I’m now checking additional same-topology parents and the four-phase CaO-retention model before accepting or deleting CaO.

The common-profile pass exposes the expected masking problem: freeing all instrumental terms can force W and Y negative, which is not an acceptable model even though it lowers Rwp. I’m rejecting that branch and reallocating the width variation to physically interpretable phase-specific size/strain terms, with the common instrument profile kept positive. The zero-only position branch is retained for now; shift-only and three joint restarts produce essentially the same fractions, but no better fit, and the joint solution is 99.9% correlated.

With physically valid common width terms held fixed, the roughness correction is strongly supported and reproducible: three starts converge to A ≈ 0.358 and B ≈ 0.37, Rwp ≈ 7.61%, and essentially identical fractions (~73.1% CaWO₄, 14.7% WO₃, 12.1% Ca₃WO₆). The remaining residual is now mostly reflection-specific intensity and anisotropic peak-shape structure, so I’m screening phase-by-phase texture and directional broadening before any coordinate refinement.

The three-phase inventory is now well supported: scheelite CaWO₄, monoclinic WO₃, and a Ca₃WO₆-type ordered double perovskite; CaO fails the deletion/significance test. I’m finishing the matched analogue swaps and the mechanism-complete profile pass before allowing any atomic-coordinate refinement.

The broad residual is reproducible across roughness starts, and phase fractions remain stable. I’ve retained the physical zero-only displacement branch, positive profile coefficients, and a modest WO₃ [001] texture term; next I’m comparing smooth background families at equal complexity, then phase-specific anisotropic broadening.

The phase-specific width test found a strong, restart-stable WO₃ anisotropy (about 0.055 µm equatorial versus 0.020 µm along b), plus a smaller symmetry-consistent CaWO₄ [001] effect. I’m now testing whether strain can replace or legitimately complement those size terms; any negative or start-dependent broadening branch will be discarded.

The profile pass now has a physically valid, restart-stable branch: positive instrumental coefficients, roughness, CaWO₄ [001] and WO₃ [010] size anisotropy, and WO₃ [001] texture. Fractions vary by only about 0.2 percentage point across the admissible instrumental-width grid. I’m applying that exact model in fresh restarts to every serious Ca₃WO₆ parent and WO₃ structure source.

The analogue swap changed the structural conclusion in an important way: an exact-composition Ca₃UO₆ parent mapped U→W outperforms the multication Sr₂MnWO₆ mapping by ΔRwp ≈ 0.45% and remains chemically/topologically closer. Both are physically screenable, so I’m retaining both through the final coordinate tests rather than selecting on that early advantage alone.

The repeated profile pass resolved the earlier negative-strain branch without forcing a bound: a small positive instrumental U=2 and a positive CaWO₄ microstrain (~160 ppm) reproduce the same curve and fractions. The Ca₃WO₆ peaks also support a much larger ~0.9% strain term, so I’m checking its start-to-start stability and its effect on fractions before deciding whether that broadening is genuinely determined.

The inventory and profile are now stable with no active bounds or frozen parameters: three positive strain terms, a reproducible roughness/polarization subspace, and only the strongly supported WO₃ texture. Atomic refinement is starting now, one element/site block at a time; Ca₃WO₆ oxygen motion is being screened with broad W–O and B-site Ca–O restraints.

The coordinate tests are separating signal from overfit cleanly. CaWO₄ supports a small oxygen shift; unconstrained WO₃ and Ca₃WO₆ site motions often create large coordinate jumps or short bonds and are being rejected. A one-parameter chemical adaptation of the U-parent oxygen cage toward 1.93 Å W–O bonds is physical and improves monotonically, so I’m comparing that fixed adapted cage against the Sr/Mn-parent finalist under the exact same final profile model.

The phase inventory and nuisance model are now stable. I’m finishing the last selective atomic-coordinate test—whether small tungsten shifts in monoclinic WO₃ survive matched restarts—then I’ll run deletion/physical screens and generate the submission files from the replayed GSAS-II project.

The WO₃ result is decisive enough to retain only the W1 coordinate block: two independent nuisance starts converge to the same coordinates and fractions (Rwp 4.895%), while moving both W sites drives the surface-roughness term negative and is rejected. I’m now checking bond geometry and the necessity of each phase against the fully equilibrated model.

The deletion tests confirm both minor phases are essential: omitting WO₃ raises Rwp to 15.27%, and omitting Ca₃WO₆ raises it to 11.17%. Re-adding CaO gives only 0.084(45) wt% and changes Rwp by 0.0012 percentage point, so it is not retained. The dominant-phase deletion is still equilibrating; I’m letting that diagnostic finish while preparing the final project state.

The roughness/polarization terms form a very flat correlated subspace, but the important quantities are stable: two physically valid starts change phase fractions by at most 0.005 wt% and give essentially identical cells/coordinates and calculated profiles. I’m selecting a positive-coefficient representative of that subspace and replaying it once more; the invalid negative-roughness branches are excluded rather than used to lower Rwp.

A final CIF audit exposed one substantive uncertainty: the historical WO₃ and Ca₃UO₆ parents encode several displacement parameters as zero. Replacing those placeholders with fixed positive Uiso values changes the phase scales by about 1 wt%, confirming that the formal scale e.s.d.’s understate model uncertainty. I’m testing whether that physically normalized branch can retain positive nuisance coefficients; if so I’ll use it and report the ADP-driven systematic explicitly.

The normalized branch succeeds with a cleaner mechanism: fixed positive Uiso = 0.01 Å² for parent sites lacking reported ADPs, roughness refined, and polarization refinement omitted because every free-polarization restart either became nonphysical or added no stable information. Two roughness restarts converge to the same curve and fractions (differences <0.003 wt%). I’m using that model for the final replay and repeating the trace-phase tests against it.

The final normalized project now replays cleanly: fractions change by less than 0.0003 wt% and all refined coefficients remain physical. Final deletion tests give Rwp 15.22% without WO₃ and 11.16% without Ca₃WO₆; CaO remains unsupported at 0.087(43) wt% with only a 0.0009-point Rwp change. I’m writing the result and uncertainty record now, including the analogue provenance and ADP sensitivity.

Resolved three crystalline phases:

- CaWO₄ scheelite: 70.17 wt%
- Monoclinic WO₃: 16.23 wt%
- Ca₃WO₆-type phase: 13.60 wt%

Final Rwp = 4.800%, goodness of fit = 1.834. The Ca₃WO₆ topology is supported, but its exact oxygen coordinates remain model-dependent. Estimated systematic fraction uncertainties are approximately 1.1, 0.9, and 0.5 wt%, respectively.

